    <h1>Private Label: What It Means</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Private label means you re-brand a manufacturer's <em>existing</em> product as your own. It is
      distinct from OEM, where the manufacturer builds a product <em>to your specification</em>. This
      page explains the concept neutrally — there is no quote process and nothing to buy here.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Private label is branding alone: the product already exists, and
      you put your name on it. OEM is product development plus branding. Private label is faster to
      launch; OEM gives you the formula. See the
      <a href="/blog/oem-vs-private-label/">OEM vs private label</a> comparison for the full picture.
    </div>

    <h2>What private label is not</h2>
    <p>
      Three distinctions keep the term precise:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Not OEM.</strong> Private label re-brands an existing product; OEM builds to your specification.</li>
      <li><strong>Not custom fragrance development.</strong> There is no brief or formulation loop — you are selecting, not commissioning.</li>
      <li><strong>Not necessarily lower quality.</strong> "Existing product" describes <em>when</em> the product was made, not <em>how well</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the arrangement splits control</h2>
    <p>
      In a private-label arrangement, the buyer brings the <strong>brand</strong> — name, logo, label,
      packaging — while the <strong>formula, material specification, and production process</strong>
      stay with the manufacturer. That division is the point of the route: each side controls what it
      is good at. If the shared-product reality is a problem — if you need a product competitors cannot
      simply re-brand — that is a reason to move toward <a href="/oem/">OEM</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The material underneath still matters</h2>
    <p>
      "Existing" does not mean "uninspected." The material is whatever the published standard for that
      product is — a botanical wood, resin, herb, or flower, not a synthetic fragrance unless stated.
      A maker who wants to verify what is actually in the product can do so against the
      <a href="/ingredients/">ingredient reference</a> and the
      <a href="/blog/incense-authentication-database/">authentication database</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For the full side-by-side comparison and the questions a knowledgeable maker asks, see the
      <a href="/blog/oem-vs-private-label/">OEM vs private label</a> explainer.
    </p>
